# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored and never enters an image.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Sandbox only unless you have said otherwise out loud. The account allows ten
# Production Items and removing one does not give the slot back — docs/PLAID.md.

# Required. The access tokens live here, so a server without a database cannot
# reach a bank at all.
#
# Where the database is, from the point of view of whatever is connecting.
# This value is for a server run on the host with `cargo run`; the server
# container overrides it in docker-compose.yml, because inside the network the
# database is called `db`.
#
# No `sslmode` here, which is a decision and not the oversight it replaced.
# The database does serve TLS — see scripts/db-certs.sh — and the deployed
# server checks it with `sslmode=verify-full`. This URL is the other kind of
# caller: host tooling on loopback, on the machine the database is running on.
# Requiring verification here would mean a fresh clone could not run `psql`,
# `scripts/db-shell.sh` or a `cargo` build that touches the database until it
# had generated certificates, to protect a socket that never leaves the
# machine. Add `?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=certs/ca.crt` if you want it —
# the certificate names `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` for exactly that.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://meridian:meridian@localhost:7110/meridian

# Must match the URL above. Compose reads them for the database container.
POSTGRES_USER=meridian
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=meridian
POSTGRES_DB=meridian

# Where a sign-in code goes: `resend` or `log`. Required, no default.
# `log` writes the code to the server log at WARN and sends nothing, which is
# a real choice rather than a fallback — a server running that way says so on
# every sign-in.
EMAIL_SEND=log

# Only read when EMAIL_SEND=resend, and both required then.
#
# `mail.meridiancash.io` is verified in Resend, so an address on it reaches
# anybody. `onboarding@resend.dev` is the fallback that needs no domain at all,
# and it only ever reaches the address the Resend account was signed up with —
# every other recipient is a 403.
#
# A bare address, with no display name. `scripts/db-shell.sh` sources this file
# with `.`, and `Meridian <noreply@…>` is a shell redirect rather than a value.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM=noreply@mail.meridiancash.io

# What the access token column is sealed with: 32 random bytes, base64.
# Required whenever DATABASE_URL is, and with no default — a default key is a
# key that ships. `scripts/db-init.sh` writes one here if this is empty.
#
# Losing it means every stored access token is scrap and every bank has to be
# relinked, so it wants backing up as carefully as the database — and not to
# the same place, or one theft has both. docs/DATABASE.md has the rest.
MERIDIAN_DB_KEY=

# Plaid. From dashboard.plaid.com/developers/keys. The secret differs between
# Sandbox and Production, so the two travel together.
PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
PLAID_SECRET=

# Required, and matched exactly: `sandbox` or `production`. There is no default
# — unset, empty or misspelled all fail the process on the way up rather than
# quietly meaning Sandbox. This is the only thing that picks now — nothing in
# the server refuses Production any more — so `production` here reaches real
# banks and spends the ten Item slots that do not come back. docs/PLAID.md.
PLAID_ENV=sandbox

# How a finished Link session reaches us: `poll` or `webhook`. Required, no
# default. A webhook needs an address Plaid can reach, which a laptop on a home
# network does not have, so development is `poll`.
#
# Not the same axis as PLAID_ENV, and deliberately not folded into it.
PLAID_COLLECT=poll

# Where this server can be reached from outside, with no trailing slash.
#
# Required when PLAID_COLLECT=webhook and read by nothing otherwise, the same
# way RESEND_* is only read when EMAIL_SEND=resend. It is what Plaid is told to
# call — `<PUBLIC_URL>/plaid/webhook` — and a process cannot work it out for
# itself: it knows the port it bound and nothing about the name in front of it.
#
# https only. Plaid will not call anything else, so an http address here is
# refused on the way up rather than accepted and never called.
PUBLIC_URL=

# Where an OAuth bank's own app hands the user back to ours, and the only
# variable here that is optional.
#
# Not the same thing as the `meridian://linked` the sheet closes on, and the
# two are not interchangeable. That one is opened by Plaid's page at the end of
# the flow; this one is opened by the *bank's* app, part way through, and so it
# has to be an https Universal Link on a domain that vouches for the app —
# `https://api.meridiancash.io/link/oauth`. A custom scheme cannot do it.
#
# Leave it empty until three things are true, or a bank hands back to nobody:
# the app is signed by a team with the associated-domains entitlement,
# MERIDIAN_TEAM_ID names that team on the box, and the address is registered in
# the Plaid dashboard. Empty is the honest answer and the link token says so.
PLAID_REDIRECT_URI=

# The Apple Developer team the phone app is signed by. Read only by Caddy on
# the box, to name the app in the file iOS reads at
# `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association`. Empty until the account exists.
MERIDIAN_TEAM_ID=

# The origins the web app is served from, comma separated. Required, with no
# default, like everything else here.
#
# This is the whole of what CORS decides, and it only ever affects a browser:
# the iOS app is not one, Plaid is not one, and neither is `curl`. On the box
# it is `https://app.meridiancash.io`; on a laptop it is trunk's port, because
# a web front-end on :7130 calling a server on :7120 is cross-origin.
#
# `http://` is refused for anything but localhost. A wrong value here is the
# one mistake this server cannot notice on its own — a browser refused by CORS
# never sends the request — so it is checked on the way up instead.
WEB_ORIGINS=http://127.0.0.1:7130

# Deploying. Only `scripts/deploy.sh` reads this, and only from a laptop; the
# box's own `.env` is a different set of answers and docs/DEPLOY.md lists them.
#
# Not committed, because it names a machine and this repo is public. Leave it
# empty and every deploy subcommand says so rather than guessing at a host.
MERIDIAN_HOST=

# Stripe, which is how a subscription is paid for on the web and the desktop.
# The phone does not use these: iOS sells through StoreKit, and what arrives
# here is Apple's word for it rather than a card.
#
# `test` or `live`, exactly, and no default. Stripe has one host for both, so
# the key alone picks which one a call lands in, and a live key works
# perfectly while charging a real card. Naming the mode here means a key from
# the other mode fails the process on the way up instead of working.
STRIPE_ENV=test

# `sk_test_` or `sk_live_`, and its prefix has to agree with STRIPE_ENV. Both
# keys are per-account: a key from one Stripe account cannot see a price from
# another, and the failure reads as "no such price" rather than as a wrong
# account.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The card form's key, which is made to be in a page. The server hands it out
# beside each subscription's secret, so no client is built with one. `pk_test_`
# or `pk_live_`, matching STRIPE_ENV.
STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# The $5.99/month recurring price. `price_`, never `prod_` — a product can
# carry several prices and a subscription is created against one of them, so
# the product id is the wrong value in the right shape. A price exists in one
# mode only, and its id does not say which, so this is the one Stripe value
# the server cannot check against STRIPE_ENV.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=

# What `stripe listen` prints on startup, or the endpoint's signing secret in
# the dashboard once there is a deployed endpoint. The two are different
# values for the same thing, and a webhook signed by one does not verify
# against the other.
#
# Leave it empty on a laptop. `scripts/dev.sh` asks the CLI for the listener's
# secret and writes it in here the first time it finds this line empty — it
# belongs to the account STRIPE_SECRET_KEY already names and is the same value
# every time, so there is nothing to go and look up. A value put here on
# purpose is never touched.
#
# Without it every webhook is refused, which is the safe direction: an
# unverified webhook is somebody claiming a subscription was paid for.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Apple, which is how a subscription is paid for on the phone. The web and the
# desktop app do not use this: they take a card through Stripe above.
#
# `sandbox` or `production`, and no default. A build that says neither refuses
# every purchase, which is the safe direction — a sandbox purchase costs
# nothing, so a production server that took one would be giving the app away
# to anybody with Xcode. There is no key beside it: Apple signs a purchase
# with a certificate chain, and the root that chain has to reach is committed
# in `crates/src-apple/certs`.
#
# Apple keeps one notification URL per environment, so the sandbox and the
# production server each get their own in App Store Connect. The path is
# `/apple/webhook` on both.
APPLE_ENV=sandbox

# The market universe: symbols the server fetches on its own initiative when
# nobody is asking it anything, one `SYMBOL WEIGHT` per line. Optional — a
# server without one still answers every /bars request, and says at startup
# that it is fetching only what users ask. The image bakes this same file in
# at /etc/meridian/universe.txt, so containers need nothing set.
MARKET_UNIVERSE=data/universe.txt

# The bookkeeper — docs/BOOKS.md. Optional everywhere: books only runs on
# the box, and a laptop pass (`python books/main.py once`) says what it is
# missing and writes what it can.
#
# A *restricted* key made in the Stripe dashboard with read access to
# balance transactions and nothing else — never STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, which can
# move money. The Google credential is a file, certs/books-google-key.json,
# not a variable.
BOOKS_STRIPE_KEY=
